: Leukopenia, anemia, thrombocytopenia, lymphadenopathy, hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis.", "Immune system disorders: Hypersensitivity syndrome.", "Psychiatric disorders: Tics, hallucinations, confusion, nightmares.", "Nervous system disorders: Ataxia, nystagmus, aseptic meningitis, choreoathetosis, extrapyramidal effects, exacerbation of Parkinson's symptoms, increased seizure frequency.", "Eye disorders: Diplopia, blurred vision, conjunctivitis.", "Hepatobiliary disorders: Liver function abnormalities, hepatic failure.", "Musculoskeletal and connective tissue disorders: Lupus-like reactions."], "effectsOnDriving": "Two studies in volunteers demonstrated that the drug had no significant effect on fine visual-motor coordination, eye movements, body sway, or subjective sedation compared to placebo.\\nNeurological adverse reactions such as dizziness and diplopia have been reported. Therefore, patients should evaluate how the treatment affects them prior to driving or operating machinery.", "pharmacodynamics": {"mechanismOfAction": "Lamotrigine is a use-dependent blocker of voltage-gated sodium channels. It inhibits sustained, rapid firing of neurons and suppresses pathological release of glutamate (an amino acid playing a key role in the generation of epileptic seizures), as well as inhibiting the bursts of action potentials that release glutamate."}, "pharmacokinetics": {"absorption": "Lamotrigine is rapidly and completely absorbed from the gut with no significant first-pass metabolism. Plasma protein binding is approximately 55%; displacement from plasma proteins is unlikely to cause toxicity.", "metabolism": "UDP-glucuronyl transferases are the enzymes identified as responsible for the metabolism of lamotrigine.", "elimination": "Elimination of lamotrigine occurs primarily via metabolism with subsequent excretion of the glucuronide-conjugated metabolite in the urine. Less than 10% of the drug is excreted unchanged in the urine. Only about 2% of the drug metabolites are excreted in the feces."}, "contraindications": ["Lamictal is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to lamotrigine or to any of the excipients of the medication."], "manufacturingInfo": {"storage": "Do not store above 30°C. Store in a dry place."}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Treatment of epilepsy:\\nAdults (over 12 years of age):\\nThe initial monotherapy dose is 25 mg once daily for 2 weeks, followed by 50 mg once daily for the next 2 weeks.\\nThen the dose should be increased, by a maximum of 50 to 100 mg every 1 to 2 weeks, until the optimal response is achieved.\\nThe usual maintenance dose to achieve an optimal response is 100 to 200 mg/day given as a single dose or in two divided doses. Some patients require 500 mg of Lamictal/day to achieve the desired response.\\nChildren (2 years to 12 years of age):\\nTypical absence seizures:\\nWeeks 1-2: 0.3 mg/kg/day (given in 1 or 2 divided doses).\\nWeeks 3-4: 0.6 mg/kg/day (given in 1 or 2 divided doses).\\nMaintenance dose: Increase by 0.6 mg/kg every 1 to 2 weeks to reach a maintenance dose of 1-10 mg/kg/day (given in 1 or 2 divided doses) up to a maximum of 200 mg/day.\\nAdjunctive therapy with valproate:\\nWeeks 1-2: 0.15 mg/kg/day (given once daily).\\nWeeks 3-4: 0.3 mg/kg/day (given once daily).\\nMaintenance dose: Increase by 0.3 mg/kg every 1 to 2 weeks to reach a maintenance dose of 1-5 mg/kg/day (given in 1 or 2 divided doses) up to a maximum of 200 mg/day.\\nAdjunctive therapy without valproate:\\nShould be used with phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, primidone, or other drugs that induce lamotrigine glucuronidation:\\nWeeks 1-2: 0.6 mg/kg/day (given in 2 divided doses).\\nWeeks 3-4: 1.2 mg/kg/day (given in 2 divided doses).\\nMaintenance dose: Increase by 1.2 mg/kg every 1 to 2 weeks to reach a maintenance dose of 5-15 mg/kg/day (given in 2 divided doses) up to a maximum of 400 mg/day.\\nShould be used with medications that do not significantly inhibit or induce lamotrigine glucuronidation:\\nWeeks 1-2: 0.3 mg/kg/day (given in 1 or 2 divided doses).\\nWeeks 3-4: 0.6 mg/kg/day (given in 1 or 2 divided doses).\\nMaintenance dose: Increase by 0.6 mg/kg every 1 to 2 weeks to reach a maintenance dose of 1-10 mg/kg/day (given in 1 or 2 divided doses) up to a maximum of 200 mg/day.\\nChildren under 2 years of age:\\nLamotrigine has not been studied as monotherapy in children under 2 years of age or as adjunctive therapy in children under 1 month of age.\\nThe safety and efficacy of lamotrigine in adjunctive therapy for partial seizures in children aged 1 month to 2 years have not been established. Therefore, Lamictal is not recommended for children under 2 years of age.\\nBipolar Disorder:\\nAdults (18 years of age and older):\\nAdjunctive therapy with glucuronidation inhibitors such as valproate:\\nWeeks 1-2: 12.5 mg (25 mg tablet taken every other day).\\nWeeks 3-4: 25 mg (once daily).\\nWeek 5: 50 mg (given in 1 or 2 divided doses daily).\\nTarget stabilization dose (Week 6): 100 mg (given in 1 or 2 divided doses daily) up to a maximum dose of 200 mg/day.\\nAdjunctive therapy with glucuronidation inducers in patients not taking valproate (this regimen should be used with phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, primidone, or other drugs that induce lamotrigine glucuronidation):\\nWeeks 1-2: 50 mg (once daily).\\nWeeks 3-4: 100 mg (given in 2 divided doses daily).\\nWeek 5: 200 mg (given in 2 divided doses daily).\\nTarget stabilization dose (Week 6): 300 mg/day, increasing to 400 mg/day if necessary in week 7 (given in 2 divided doses daily).\\nMonotherapy with Lamictal or adjunctive therapy in patients taking other drugs that do not significantly inhibit or induce lamotrigine glucuronidation:\\nWeeks 1-2: 25 mg (once daily).\\nWeeks 3-4: 50 mg (given in 1 or 2 divided doses daily).\\nWeek 5: 100 mg (given in 1 or 2 divided doses daily).\\nTarget stabilization dose (Week 6): 200 mg (range 100 to 400 mg) (given in 1 or 2 divided doses daily).\\nChildren and adolescents (under 18 years of age):\\nNot recommended as safety and efficacy have not been established in this age group.\\nRenal impairment:\\nPlasma concentrations of lamotrigine were not significantly altered in single-dose studies in end-stage renal failure patients. However, accumulation of the glucuronide metabolite is anticipated; caution should be exercised in treating patients with renal impairment.\\nNote: The dosages above are for reference only. Specific dosing depends on the patient's physical condition and disease progression. Consult a physician or healthcare professional for an appropriate dosage.", "missedDose": "Take the missed dose as soon as remembered. However, if it is almost time for the next dose, skip the missed dose and resume the normal dosing schedule. Do not take a double dose to make up for a forgotten dose.", "administration": "Tablets should be swallowed whole and should not be chewed or crushed.\\nIf the calculated dose cannot be divided into lower-strength tablets (e.g., for young children with epilepsy or patients with hepatic impairment), the dose should be rounded to the nearest lower available tablet strength.", "overdoseManagement": "Acute ingestions of doses ranging from 10 to 20 times the maximum therapeutic dose have been reported, including fatal cases.\\nSymptoms of overdose include nystagmus, ataxia, altered consciousness, grand mal convulsions, and coma. QRS widening has also been observed in overdose patients.\\nIn case of overdose, the patient should be hospitalized and receive appropriate supportive care based on clinical manifestations or as directed by the national poison control center."}, "detailedIngredients": {"activeIngredients": "Lamotrigine 25mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "Lamotrigine passes into breast milk in highly variable amounts, resulting in infant serum lamotrigine concentrations of up to approximately 50% of the mother's serum concentration.\\nConsequently, in some breastfed infants, serum concentrations of lamotrigine may reach levels where pharmacological effects occur. The benefits of breastfeeding should be weighed against the potential risk of adverse effects in the infant.", "pediatric": "Lamotrigine has not been studied as monotherapy in children under 2 years of age or as adjunctive therapy in children under 1 month of age. The safety and efficacy of lamotrigine in adjunctive therapy for partial seizures in children aged 1 month to 2 years have not been established. Therefore, Lamictal is not recommended for children under 2 years of age.", "pregnancy": "Postmarketing data from more than 8,700 women exposed to lamotrigine monotherapy during the first trimester of pregnancy do not suggest a substantial increase in the risk of major congenital malformations, although data from some registries noted an increased risk of isolated oral clefts, whereas a completed case-control study showed no increased risk compared to other major birth defects.\\nLamictal should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk.\\nPhysiological changes during pregnancy may affect lamotrigine concentrations and/or therapeutic efficacy. Decreased lamotrigine blood levels during pregnancy have been reported. Appropriate clinical management should be ensured for pregnant women receiving Lamictal."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Skin rashes have commonly been reported within 8 weeks of starting Lamictal treatment, with the majority being mild and self-limiting. However, serious rashes requiring hospitalization, such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis, have also been reported.", "Caution should be exercised when administering the drug to patients with a history of allergy or rash to other antiepileptic drugs.", "Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis has been reported in patients taking Lamictal.", "Depressive symptoms and/or bipolar disorder can occur in patients with epilepsy, and there is evidence of an increased risk of suicide in patients with epilepsy and bipolar disorder.", "Patients being treated for bipolar disorder with Lamictal should be closely monitored for clinical worsening (including development of new symptoms) and suicidal ideation.", "Patients taking combined oral contraceptives generally require a higher maintenance dose of lamotrigine (up to double) to achieve maximal therapeutic response. Furthermore, Lamictal may decrease the efficacy of hormonal contraceptives in some patients.", "Lamictal inhibits renal tubular secretion via OCT 2 proteins; co-administration with primary OCT 2 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index, such as dofetilide, is not recommended.", "Brugada-type ECG changes have been observed in patients treated with the drug. Caution is advised when prescribing to patients with Brugada syndrome.", "Abrupt discontinuation of Lamictal may provoke rebound seizures. Lamictal dosage should be tapered gradually over a period of 2 weeks unless safety concerns (such as a rash) require immediate withdrawal."], "adverseReactionManagement": "If adverse reactions occur, discontinue use and notify a physician or go to the nearest medical facility for prompt management."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:49:52.768	lamictal-25mg-lamotrigine-tablets-for-epilepsy-and-bipolar-disorder-3-blisters-x-10-tablets	\N
99	Clamoxyl 250mg Oral Powder for Suspension for Bacterial Infections (Box of 12 Sachets)	P00605	\N	\N	Glaxo Wellcome Production	France	72273.00	63600.00	https://prod-cdn.pharmacity.io/e-com/images/ecommerce/20250728074531-0-P00605.png?versionId=S.6NyMjnuTU2O7eypjxJATVOA1VibftN	Clamoxyl 250mg, containing amoxicillin, is an oral powder for suspension indicated for the treatment of susceptible bacterial infections involving the respiratory, gastrointestinal, and genitourinary tracts, Lyme disease, and endocarditis prophylaxis, as well as component of H. pylori eradication therapy.	{"indications": ["Treatment of infections caused by susceptible pathogens in the upper and lower respiratory tracts, gastrointestinal tract, and genitourinary tract", "Lyme disease", "Endocarditis prophylaxis", "Skin and soft tissue infections, osteomyelitis, and infections of the gallbladder and biliary tract", "Severe systemic bacterial infections", "Adjunctive therapy in combination with proton pump inhibitors and other antimicrobial agents for Helicobacter pylori eradication"], "sideEffects": ["Blood and lymphatic system disorders: Leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, hemolytic anemia, prolongation of bleeding time and prothrombin time", "Immune system disorders: Hypersensitivity reactions, angioneurotic edema, anaphylactic shock, serum sickness, hypersensitivity vasculitis", "Nervous system disorders: Hyperkinesia, dizziness, aseptic convulsions", "Infections and infestations: Mucocutaneous candidiasis", "Gastrointestinal disorders: Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, antibiotic-associated colitis (including pseudomembranous colitis), black hairy tongue", "Hepatobiliary disorders: Hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice, transient elevation of serum transaminases (AST and ALT)", "Skin and subcutaneous tissue disorders: Maculopapular rash, urticaria, pruritus, erythema multiforme, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis, bullous exfoliative dermatitis, acute generalized exanthematous pustulosis (AGEP)", "Renal and urinary disorders: Interstitial nephritis, crystalluria"], "drugInteractions": [{"drug": "Probenecid", "effect": "Decreases renal tubular secretion of amoxicillin, resulting in increased and prolonged serum concentrations."}, {"drug": "Allopurinol", "effect": "Concurrent use increases the incidence of cutaneous rash and hypersensitivity reactions."}, {"drug": "Tetracyclines and other bacteriostatic agents", "effect": "May antagonize the bactericidal efficacy of amoxicillin."}, {"drug": "Oral contraceptives", "effect": "May reduce the efficacy of estrogen-containing oral contraceptives, potentially leading to breakthrough bleeding or contraceptive failure."}, {"drug": "Acenocoumarol or warfarin", "effect": "May abnormally prolong prothrombin time (elevated INR); appropriate monitoring is recommended when co-administered."}], "effectsOnDriving": "No known or observed effects on the ability to drive or operate machinery.", "contraindications": ["Contraindicated in patients with a known history of hypersensitivity to beta-lactam antibiotics (penicillins and cephalosporins)."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "GSK", "origin": "France", "storage": "Store below 30°C in a dry place", "packaging": "Box of 12 sachets", "manufacturer": "Glaxo Wellcome Production"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Dosage is determined by patient age, body weight, renal function, and infection severity. The standard dosing regimen is divided into 3 administrations daily. Adults and pediatric patients weighing over 40 kg: 750 mg to 3 g daily (maximum 6 g daily). Pediatric patients weighing under 40 kg: 20 to 50 mg/kg/day (maximum 150 mg/kg/day). Renal dosage adjustment is mandatory in patients with renal impairment and those undergoing hemodialysis.", "administration": "For oral administration. Absorption of amoxicillin is not significantly impaired by the presence of food.", "overdoseManagement": "Symptoms: Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, fluid and electrolyte imbalance, and amoxicillin-induced crystalluria potentially progressing to renal failure. Treatment: Supportive and symptomatic care; amoxicillin is hemodialyzable."}, "generalDescription": "Clamoxyl 250mg, containing amoxicillin as the active pharmaceutical ingredient, is a broad-spectrum penicillin-class antibacterial indicated for the treatment of susceptible bacterial infections of the upper and lower respiratory tracts, gastrointestinal and genitourinary tracts, skin and soft tissues, bones, and biliary tract, as well as for Lyme disease, endocarditis prophylaxis, and H. pylori eradication regimens.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Crospovidone, peach-lemon-strawberry flavoring agent, aspartame, magnesium stearate, lactose", "activeIngredients": "Amoxicillin 250 mg (supplied as amoxicillin trihydrate)"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "Dosing adjustment is generally not required unless accompanied by compromised renal function.", "lactation": "Amoxicillin is excreted in breast milk in small quantities; use with caution and monitor the breastfed infant for potential sensitization or gastrointestinal disturbances.", "pregnancy": "Use only if clearly indicated after a formal risk-benefit assessment by the treating physician."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["A comprehensive clinical evaluation regarding prior hypersensitivity reactions to penicillins or cephalosporins must be conducted prior to initiation.", "Discontinue therapy immediately and institute emergency management if severe hypersensitivity or anaphylactic reactions occur.", "Avoid use if infectious mononucleosis is clinically suspected, due to the high incidence of rash associated with amoxicillin use in these patients.", "Prolonged or repeated therapy may result in overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms or superinfection.", "Monitor for signs of pseudomembranous colitis in patients presenting with severe or persistent diarrhea.", "Exercise clinical caution and monitor coagulation parameters when co-administered with oral anticoagulants.", "Ensure adequate hydration and urinary output to minimize the risk of amoxicillin-associated crystalluria.", "Contains aspartame; use with caution in patients with phenylketonuria (PKU)."], "adverseReactionManagement": "Report any unexpected adverse reactions or hypersensitivity symptoms to a qualified healthcare professional promptly."}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:18:59.977	clamoxyl-250mg-oral-powder-for-suspension-for-bacterial-infections-box-of-12-sachets	246
