butazone, warfarin, hydrocortisone, prednisolone, cyclosporine, clofibrate, theophylline, morphine, salicylic acid, temazepam, atorvastatin, acetaminophen, ascorbic acid, St. John's Wort (Hypericum perforatum)", "effect": "May diminish contraceptive efficacy or alter the pharmacokinetic profile of co-administered agents."}], "effectsOnDriving": "No formal studies on the effects on the ability to drive and use machines have been performed; however, patients should exercise caution if experiencing adverse reactions such as dizziness or visual disturbances.", "contraindications": ["Vascular disease, thromboembolic disorders, coagulation abnormalities, active thrombosis.", "Cardiovascular disease, cerebrovascular hemorrhage.", "Known or suspected hormone-sensitive malignancies, including breast cancer, cervical cancer, endometrial cancer, and uterine cancer.", "Severe hepatic impairment, benign or malignant hepatic neoplasms.", "History of cholestatic jaundice during pregnancy or prior use of combined hormonal contraceptives.", "Severe renal impairment, adrenal insufficiency.", "Undiagnosed abnormal uterine or vaginal bleeding.", "Known or suspected pregnancy.", "Hypersensitivity to drospirenone, ethinyl estradiol, or any excipients contained within the formulation."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Abbott", "storage": "Store in the original packaging at room temperature, in a dry place, protected from light.", "packaging": "Carton containing 1 blister pack of 28 film-coated tablets (comprising 21 active beige tablets and 7 inactive white tablets).", "manufacturer": "Abbott"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "One tablet daily. Management of missed doses: If one active tablet is delayed by less than 12 hours, contraceptive protection is not reduced; take the missed tablet as soon as remembered and continue subsequent tablets at the usual time. If one active tablet is delayed by more than 12 hours, contraceptive protection may be reduced: take the missed tablet immediately (even if it means taking two tablets simultaneously) and use back-up barrier contraception for the next 7 days. If two consecutive active tablets are missed during Week 1 or Week 2, take two tablets on the day they are remembered and two on the following day, then continue with barrier contraception for 7 days. If two consecutive active tablets are missed in Week 3 or more than three tablets are missed across the first three weeks, discard the remaining blister pack, initiate a new pack immediately, and utilize barrier contraception for 7 days.", "administration": "Administer one tablet orally once daily at the same time each day for 28 consecutive days in the sequential order indicated on the blister pack housed within the compact case. Initiation begins with the first beige active tablet on Day 1 of the menstrual cycle. Upon completion of the active beige tablets, continue with the inactive white tablets.", "overdoseManagement": "In the event of acute overdosage, symptoms may include nausea, vomiting, and withdrawal bleeding in females. Management should consist of symptomatic and supportive care under medical supervision."}, "generalDescription": "Drosperin tablets, manufactured by Abbott, contain drospirenone and ethinyl estradiol as active pharmaceutical ingredients. This medicinal product is indicated for daily oral contraception, postmenopausal hormone replacement therapy, and the management of menstrual disorders such as amenorrhea and oligomenorrhea resulting from ovarian dysfunction.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Active beige tablets excipients: maize starch, povidone, lactose monohydrate, croscarmellose sodium, talc, magnesium stearate, hypromellose 2910, macrogol 6000, titanium dioxide, iron oxide yellow. Inactive white tablets excipients: microcrystalline cellulose, sodium starch glycolate, lactose monohydrate, talc, magnesium stearate, hypromellose 2910, macrogol 6000, titanium dioxide.", "activeIngredients": "Each active beige film-coated tablet contains 3 mg of drospirenone and 0.03 mg (30 mcg) of ethinyl estradiol."}, "specialPatientGroups": {"lactation": "Not recommended during lactation, as combined hormonal contraceptives may reduce breast milk production and trace amounts of active steroids excreted into breast milk may adversely affect the nursing infant.", "pregnancy": "Contraindicated during pregnancy. If pregnancy occurs or is suspected during administration, discontinue treatment immediately and consult a physician."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["Utilize additional non-hormonal contraception during the first 7 days of initial cycle administration, following missed doses, or when co-administered with enzyme-inducing medicinal products that reduce contraceptive efficacy.", "Tobacco smoking cessation is strongly advised during treatment with combined hormonal contraceptives, particularly in women over 35 years of age.", "Minimize prolonged ultraviolet or sun exposure due to the potential for photosensitivity reactions.", "Discontinue treatment and inform attending physicians or dental surgeons prior to elective scheduled surgeries or immobilization periods.", "In the event of severe gastrointestinal disturbances (e.g., vomiting or acute diarrhea), absorption may be incomplete, and supplementary barrier contraception should be employed.", "Routine clinical evaluations and gynecological monitoring are required during long-term therapy."]}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:21:12.866	drosperin-drospirenone-3-mg-ethinyl-estradiol-0-03-mg-film-coated-tablets-for-oral-contraception	3
