ssium-sparing diuretics (spironolactone, triamterene, etc.), potassium supplements", "effect": "Elevated serum potassium levels."}, {"drug": "Aliskiren fumarate", "effect": "Risk of renal impairment, hyperkalemia, and hypotension."}, {"drug": "Antihypertensive diuretics (trichlormethiazide, hydrochlorothiazide, etc.)", "effect": "Pronounced hypotension following the initiation of imidapril."}, {"drug": "Lithium-containing preparations (lithium carbonate)", "effect": "Risk of lithium toxicity (somnolence, tremor, confusion, etc.)."}, {"drug": "Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (e.g., indomethacin)", "effect": "Reduced antihypertensive efficacy of imidapril."}, {"drug": "Kallidinogenase-containing preparations", "effect": "Excessive blood pressure reduction."}, {"drug": "Antihypertensive agents (nitrates, etc.)", "effect": "Potentiated hypotensive effect."}, {"drug": "Non-potassium-sparing diuretics", "effect": "Risk of sudden and severe hypotension and/or acute renal failure upon initiating ACE inhibitor therapy."}, {"drug": "Gold preparations", "effect": "Nitritoid reactions (flushing, nausea, vomiting, hypotension)."}, {"drug": "Antidiabetic medications (insulin, sulfonamides)", "effect": "Enhanced hypoglycemic effect."}, {"drug": "Tricyclic antidepressants, antipsychotics", "effect": "Enhanced hypotensive effect and increased risk of orthostatic hypotension."}, {"drug": "Rifampicin", "effect": "Decreased serum concentrations of imidapril and its active metabolite, potentially reducing the antihypertensive effect."}, {"drug": "Sympathomimetics", "effect": "Reduced antihypertensive efficacy."}], "effectsOnDriving": "Due to the hypotensive effect of the medication which may cause dizziness or lightheadedness, patients should exercise caution when engaging in activities requiring alertness, such as driving, working at heights, or operating machinery.", "contraindications": ["Patients with a history of hypersensitivity to any component of the medication.", "Patients with a history of angioedema (angioedema associated with ACE inhibitors, hereditary angioedema, acquired angioedema, idiopathic angioedema, etc., which may present with dyspnea).", "Patients undergoing extracorporeal treatment using dextran sulfate fixed with cellulose, tryptophan fixed with polyvinyl alcohol, or polyethylene terephthalate (risk of shock).", "Patients undergoing hemodialysis with acrylonitrile methallylsulfonate sodium (AN69) membranes due to the risk of anaphylactoid reactions.", "Women who are pregnant or of childbearing potential.", "Patients with diabetes mellitus receiving treatment with aliskiren fumarate (except in patients whose blood pressure remains uncontrolled despite alternative antihypertensive therapy). Increased risk of non-fatal stroke, hyperkalemia, and renal impairment has been reported.", "Renal impairment with or without hemodialysis (creatinine clearance < 10 mL/min)."], "manufacturingInfo": {"brand": "Tanatril", "origin": "[object Object]", "storage": "Store in a dry place below 30°C. Protect from light. Protect from moisture after opening the box.", "packaging": "[object Object]", "manufacturer": "[object Object]"}, "usageInstructions": {"dosage": "Adults: Initial dose is 5 mg once daily. If optimal blood pressure is not achieved after 3 weeks of treatment, the daily dose may be increased to 10 mg, which is the optimal daily dose. However, a small number of patients may require a maximum recommended daily dose of 20 mg, or combination therapy with a diuretic. The benefits of combining imidapril with other antihypertensive modalities have not been fully evaluated in all patients.\\nElderly patients (65 years and older): The initial dose is 2.5 mg once daily. Dosage should be adjusted based on blood pressure response. The maximum recommended dose is 10 mg once daily.\\nRenal impairment: Imidapril and its active pharmacological metabolite imidaprilat are primarily eliminated via the kidneys. Renal function should be assessed prior to initiating imidapril therapy in patients with suspected renal impairment. Creatinine clearance should be determined prior to treatment using the Cockcroft & Gault formula (Nephron 1976, 16: 31-41).\\nHepatic impairment: Recommended initial dose is 2.5 mg once daily. Exercise caution when prescribing this medication to this patient group.\\nPatients at increased risk of first-dose hypotension: First-dose hypotension may occur in high-risk patients. Prior to initiating treatment, correct salt and/or fluid depletion if possible, and discontinue diuretic therapy 2 to 3 days before starting ACE inhibitor treatment. If correction is not feasible, an initial dose of 2.5 mg is recommended. In patients with severe congestive heart failure, hypotension has been reported following ACE inhibitor therapy. In these patients, the recommended dose is 2.5 mg once daily under strict medical supervision. Patients at high risk of acute, severe hypotension after the first dose should be medically monitored, preferably in a hospital setting, for up to 68 hours following the initial dose and whenever the dose of imidapril or a concomitant diuretic is increased. The starting dose should be 2.5 mg. This also applies to patients with angina pectoris or cerebrovascular disease, who are at increased risk of myocardial infarction or cerebrovascular accidents due to excessive blood pressure reduction.\\nPediatric patients: Safety and efficacy of Tanatril have not been established due to insufficient research data.", "missedDose": "[object Object]", "administration": "The medication should be taken at the same time each day approximately 15 minutes before a meal, as clinical efficacy has been established under these conditions.", "overdoseManagement": "[object Object]"}, "generalDescription": "Tanatril 5mg tablets are an antihypertensive medication containing the active ingredient imidapril hydrochloride. The medication is used for the treatment of essential hypertension in adults.", "detailedIngredients": {"excipients": "Lactose, Macrogol 6000, Magnesium stearate", "activeIngredients": "Imidapril hydrochloride 5mg"}, "specialPatientGroups": {"geriatric": "Special caution must be exercised when administering the medication to this population, initiating at a lower dose (e.g., 2.5 mg) and monitoring the patient carefully. Tanatril is primarily eliminated via the kidneys, and elderly patients often have impaired renal function, leading to higher drug concentrations and increased susceptibility to adverse reactions. Additionally, excessive hypotension is dangerous for elderly patients (cerebral infarction, etc.).", "lactation": "Do not use Tanatril during lactation. If treatment is necessary, breast-feeding must be discontinued.", "pediatric": "The safety and efficacy of Tanatril have not been established due to insufficient research data.", "pregnancy": "Tanatril is contraindicated during pregnancy or suspected pregnancy due to the risk of oligohydramnios, fetal death, hypotension, renal failure, hyperkalemia, and neonatal skull hypoplasia during the second or third trimesters. Limb contractures and craniofacial deformations in newborns may occur secondary to oligohydramnios."}, "warningsAndPrecautions": ["General precautions (Tanatril must be used with caution in the following patients):", "Renal impairment: Changes in renal function can be anticipated in susceptible individuals due to the inhibition of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. Therefore, imidapril and other ACE inhibitors should be used with caution in patients with renal impairment. Dosage reduction is required for patients with a creatinine clearance between 30 and 80 mL/min.", "Imidapril should not be used in patients with a creatinine clearance below 30 mL/min due to limited clinical experience.", "Monitor renal function closely throughout treatment. Renal impairment has been reported in association with ACE inhibitors, primarily in patients with severe heart failure or underlying renal disease, including renal artery stenosis. Some patients without prior renal disease may experience transient increases in blood urea nitrogen and serum creatinine when ACE inhibitors are co-administered with diuretics. Dosage reduction of the ACE inhibitor and/or diuretic may be required, and renal function should be monitored during the first week of treatment.", "Unilateral or bilateral renal artery stenosis.", "Cerebrovascular disorders (severe hypotension may cause reduced arterial blood flow to the brain, worsening the patient's condition).", "Elderly patients.", "Hyperkalemia.", "Important precautions:", "Patient populations including those with severe hypertension, patients undergoing hemodialysis, patients taking diuretics (especially newly initiated diuretic therapy), or patients on a strict salt-restricted diet may experience a transient, profound hypotensive drop following the initial dose of Tanatril; treatment should be initiated at a lower dose with gradual titration while closely monitoring the patient.", "Discontinue the medication 24 hours prior to surgery.", "In patients with unilateral or bilateral renal artery stenosis, renal function may rapidly deteriorate due to decreased renal blood flow and reduced glomerular filtration pressure. Tanatril should not be used in these patients unless treatment is strictly necessary.", "In patients with hyperkalemia, symptoms may worsen. Tanatril should not be used in these patients unless treatment is strictly necessary. Since hyperkalemia is often secondary to renal impairment and/or uncontrolled diabetes mellitus, serum potassium levels should be monitored carefully.", "Patients may experience renal impairment, hyperkalemia, and hypotension when combining the medication with aliskiren fumarate. Do not co-administer Tanatril and aliskiren fumarate in patients with renal impairment with an estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) < 60 mL/min/1.73 m - , unless necessary.", "In patients with type 1 diabetes mellitus-associated diabetic nephropathy, further deterioration of renal function or hyperkalemia may occur during the initial treatment period (approximately 1 month). Serum creatinine and potassium levels should be monitored throughout the initiation phase. If rapid renal function decline or severe hyperkalemia occurs, reduce the dose or discontinue the medication.", "Aortic stenosis or mitral stenosis/hypertrophic cardiomyopathy: Similar to other ACE inhibitors, exercise caution when prescribing imidapril to patients with left ventricular outflow obstruction.", "Neutropenia/agranulocytosis, thrombocytopenia, and anemia are rarely observed in patients treated with ACE inhibitors, including imidapril. In patients with normal renal function and no other risk factors, neutropenia is extremely rare. Use imidapril with extreme caution in patients with collagen vascular disease, immunosuppressive therapy, treatment with allopurinol or procainamide, or those with multiple risk factors, particularly pre-existing renal impairment. In rare isolated cases, some patients with severe infections fail to respond to potent antibiotics.", "If imidapril is administered to such patients, white blood cell counts and differentials should be monitored prior to treatment, every 2 weeks during the first 3 months of imidapril therapy, and periodically thereafter. Throughout treatment, patients should be instructed to report any signs of infection (e.g., pharyngitis, fever) to facilitate detection of leukopenia.", "Discontinue imidapril and concomitant medications if neutropenia is detected or suspected (neutrophil count below 1000/mm - ). In most patients, neutrophil counts return to normal upon discontinuation of imidapril.", "Hypertension secondary to renovascular disease: Increased risk of severe hypotension and renal failure in patients with bilateral or unilateral renal artery stenosis treated with ACE inhibitors. Loss of renal function may occur with only minor changes in serum creatinine. In these patients, treatment must be initiated under close medical supervision at low doses, with strict monitoring of renal function.", "Kidney transplantation: There is no clinical experience with the administration of imidapril in recent kidney transplant recipients.", "Anaphylactoid reactions during desensitization: Life-threatening anaphylactoid reactions rarely occur in patients receiving insect venom desensitization therapy while taking another ACE inhibitor. In these patients, such reactions can be avoided by temporarily withholding the ACE inhibitor. Exercise caution when administering ACE inhibitors to patients undergoing anesthesia.", "Hepatic impairment: Rarely, ACE inhibitors have been associated with a syndrome that starts with cholestatic jaundice and progresses to fulminant hepatic necrosis and (sometimes) death. The mechanism of this syndrome is not fully understood. Patients receiving ACE inhibitors who develop jaundice or marked elevations of hepatic enzymes should discontinue the ACE inhibitor and receive appropriate medical follow-up.", "Diabetic patients: Blood glucose levels should be closely monitored in pre-existing diabetic patients treated with oral hypoglycemic agents or insulin during the first month of ACE inhibitor therapy.", "Lactose: Tanatril contains lactose. Patients with rare hereditary problems of galactose intolerance, total lactase deficiency, or glucose-galactose malabsorption should not take this medication.", "Dual blockade of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS): There is evidence that co-administration of ACE inhibitors, angiotensin II receptor blockers, or aliskiren increases the risk of hypotension, hyperkalemia, and renal impairment (including renal failure). Dual blockade of the RAAS through the combination of ACE inhibitors, angiotensin II receptor blockers, or aliskiren is not recommended. If dual blockade therapy is considered absolutely necessary, it must be subject to close supervision and frequent monitoring of renal function, electrolytes, and blood pressure. ACE inhibitors and angiotensin II receptor blockers should not be co-administered in patients with diabetic nephropathy.", "Elderly patients: Special caution must be exercised when administering the medication to this population; initiate therapy at a lower dose (e.g., 2.5 mg) and monitor the patient carefully. Tanatril is primarily eliminated via the kidneys, and elderly patients frequently exhibit decreased renal function, leading to elevated drug retention and an increased susceptibility to adverse reactions. Furthermore, excessive blood pressure reduction poses severe risks to elderly patients (e.g., cerebral infarction).", "Pediatric use: The safety of the medication in children has not been established.", "Caution: Tanatril tablets are packaged in press-through blister (PTP) packs. Patients must be instructed to remove the medication from the packaging before ingestion (cases have been reported where patients accidentally swallowed the entire PTP pack, and the sharp edges of the foil lacerated the esophageal mucosa, leading to serious complications such as mediastinitis)."]}	t	ACTIVE	2026-04-08 21:49:38.223	tanatril-5mg-tablets-for-essential-hypertension-10-blisters-x-10-tablets	\N
